(_jl.chapters = _jl.chapters || []).push(
  { n:1, summary:"Joel son of Pethuel delivers the word of the LORD: an unprecedented disaster has struck the land. Waves of destroying insects have stripped everything — what the palmerworm left, the locust ate; what the locust left, the cankerworm and caterpiller consumed — leaving vine, fig tree, and every field bare, so corn, new wine, and oil are gone. Joel calls the drunkards to weep, the farmers to be ashamed, and the priests to lament in sackcloth, because the meat offering and the drink offering are cut off from the house of the LORD. He urges everyone to sanctify a fast, call a solemn assembly, gather in the temple, and cry unto the LORD, for the day of the LORD is at hand, coming like destruction from the Almighty. Even the cattle groan for want of pasture, and fire devours the pastures while the rivers of waters dry up.",
    context:"The book's opening names only Joel the son of Pethuel as the receiver of the word of the LORD and gives no further date or setting beyond the crisis itself.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"Joel 1:4", text:"That which the palmerworm hath left hath the locust eaten; and that which the locust hath left hath the cankerworm eaten; and that which the cankerworm hath left hath the caterpiller eaten." },
      { ref:"Joel 1:14", text:"Sanctify ye a fast, call a solemn assembly, gather the elders and all the inhabitants of the land into the house of the LORD your God, and cry unto the LORD," },
      { ref:"Joel 1:15", text:"Alas for the day! for the day of the LORD is at hand, and as a destruction from the Almighty shall it come." }
    ],
    takeaway:"When devastation strips every comfort away, the right response is to turn to the Lord together in fasting, mourning, and prayer.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"Which four destroying creatures does Joel name as eating everything in waves?", choices:["The palmerworm, the locust, the cankerworm, and the caterpiller","The fly, the beetle, the ant, and the hornet","The lion, the bear, the leopard, and the wolf","Hail, frost, drought, and mildew"], a:0, why:"Joel 1:4 lists four destroying insects, each one finishing what the previous left." },
      { q:"Why does Joel tell the priests to lament and lie all night in sackcloth?", choices:["The temple treasury had been emptied by robbers","The meat offering and the drink offering were cut off from the house of the LORD","They had been forbidden to offer any more sacrifices","The king had closed the temple for the season"], a:1, why:"Joel 1:9 and 1:13 tie the priests' mourning to the offerings being withheld from the house of God." },
      { q:"What does Joel 1 show is the right response when disaster strips a community bare?", choices:["Blame the neighbors and retaliate","Pretend life is going on as usual","Gather everyone into the house of the LORD with a fast and cry unto Him","Stockpile what remains and wait it out"], a:2, why:"Joel 1:14 calls for a sanctified fast and solemn assembly in the house of the LORD, with all the inhabitants crying unto the LORD." }
    ] },
  { n:2, summary:"Trumpets sound the alarm in Zion because the day of the LORD is near — a day of darkness led by a great, disciplined army that runs like mighty men, climbs walls, enters windows like a thief, and turns the land into an Eden ahead of it and a desolate wilderness behind. Yet before that terror the LORD pleads for return: turn to Him with all your heart, with fasting, weeping, and mourning; rend your heart and not your garments, for He is gracious and merciful, slow to anger, and of great kindness. The people gather in a solemn assembly while the priests plead for the LORD to spare His people. Then He becomes jealous for His land: the northern army is driven off, corn, wine, and oil return, and He promises to restore the years the locust hath eaten. Afterward He will pour out His spirit upon all flesh, and whosoever calls on the name of the LORD shall be delivered.",
    context:"The alarm centers on Zion and Jerusalem as the LORD calls His people to wholehearted repentance and promises restoration followed by an outpouring of His spirit.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"Joel 2:13", text:"And rend your heart, and not your garments, and turn unto the LORD your God: for he is gracious and merciful, slow to anger, and of great kindness, and repenteth him of the evil." },
      { ref:"Joel 2:25", text:"And I will restore to you the years that the locust hath eaten, the cankerworm, and the caterpiller, and the palmerworm, my great army which I sent among you." },
      { ref:"Joel 2:28", text:"And it shall come to pass afterward, that I will pour out my spirit upon all flesh; and your sons and your daughters shall prophesy, your old men shall dream dreams, your young men shall see visions:" }
    ],
    takeaway:"The Lord asks for torn hearts, not torn garments — genuine repentance meets a God who is gracious, merciful, and ready to restore.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"According to Joel 2:25, what did the LORD promise to restore to His people?", choices:["The gold carried off by raiders","The years that the locust, the cankerworm, the caterpiller, and the palmerworm had eaten","The ten tribes carried into Assyria","The temple vessels lost in the invasion"], a:1, why:"Joel 2:25 names the locust, cankerworm, caterpiller, and palmerworm — the LORD's own great army — and vows to restore the years they ate." },
      { q:"What did the LORD ask for instead of torn garments?", choices:["A costly tribute of corn, wine, and oil","New robes for the solemn assembly","Hearts turned to Him in genuine repentance, with fasting, weeping, and mourning","Silence while the priests prayed alone"], a:2, why:"Joel 2:12-13 calls for turning to the LORD with all the heart, with fasting, weeping, and mourning, and for rending the heart rather than the garments." },
      { q:"Joel 2:32 promises deliverance to whom?", choices:["Only the tribe of Judah","Only those who never joined the idolatry","Only the priests at the altar","Whosoever shall call on the name of the LORD"], a:3, why:"Joel 2:32 says whosoever shall call on the name of the LORD shall be delivered, with deliverance in mount Zion and Jerusalem." }
    ] },
  { n:3, summary:"When the LORD brings again the captivity of Judah and Jerusalem, He gathers all the nations into the valley of Jehoshaphat to plead with them for scattering His people, casting lots over them, and selling boys and girls like merchandise. Tyre, Zidon, and the coasts of Palestine are singled out for taking the temple's silver and gold and selling the children of Judah to the Grecians; their recompence will return upon their own heads. The nations are told to prepare for war — beating plowshares into swords and pruninghooks into spears — but the LORD sits as judge, and their wickedness is a ripe harvest for the sickle. Multitudes crowd the valley of decision as the day of the LORD draws near. Then the LORD roars out of Zion as the hope of His people: Jerusalem becomes holy, a fountain flows from the house of the LORD, Egypt and Edom lie desolate, and Judah dwells for ever.",
    context:"The oracle looks ahead to the LORD's judgment of the nations that scattered Judah and to a holy, restored Jerusalem with the LORD dwelling in Zion.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"Joel 3:10", text:"Beat your plowshares into swords, and your pruninghooks into spears: let the weak say, I am strong." },
      { ref:"Joel 3:14", text:"Multitudes, multitudes in the valley of decision: for the day of the LORD is near in the valley of decision." },
      { ref:"Joel 3:16", text:"The LORD also shall roar out of Zion, and utter his voice from Jerusalem; and the heavens and the earth shall shake: but the LORD will be the hope of his people, and the strength of the children of Israel." }
    ],
    takeaway:"God sees every injustice done to His people, and He Himself will judge the nations and set all wrongs right in His time.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"Into what valley does the LORD gather all nations for judgment in Joel 3?", choices:["The valley of Jehoshaphat, called the valley of decision","The valley of Elah","The valley of Achor","The valley of dry bones"], a:0, why:"Joel 3:2 names the valley of Jehoshaphat, and verse 14 calls it the valley of decision." },
      { q:"What does the command to beat plowshares into swords reveal about that day?", choices:["The nations were being told to become farmers","Peace treaties had finally been signed","The nations are summoned to war, and the LORD will meet them there in judgment","The harvest had failed and tools were worthless"], a:2, why:"Joel 3:9-12 proclaims prepare war among the Gentiles and gathers the heathen to the valley where the LORD sits to judge them." },
      { q:"The closing promise that Judah shall dwell for ever with the LORD in Zion teaches what about God's ultimate purpose for His people?", choices:["Endless safety and holiness in His presence","A return to life under the old plowshares","Dominion over Egypt and Edom by force","Rest from worship once the nations are judged"], a:3, why:"Joel 3:17, 20-21 promises the LORD dwelling in Zion, a holy Jerusalem, and Judah dwelling for ever." }
    ] }
);
(_am.chapters = _am.chapters || []).push(
  { n:1, summary:"Amos, a herdsman of Tekoa, delivers words he saw concerning Israel in the days of Uzziah king of Judah and Jeroboam the son of Joash king of Israel, two years before the earthquake. The LORD will roar from Zion; then the sentence rolls nation by nation: for three transgressions and for four, I will not turn away the punishment thereof. Damascus is condemned for threshing Gilead with threshing instruments of iron; Gaza and Tyrus for delivering whole captive populations to Edom, Tyrus forgetting the brotherly covenant; Edom for pursuing his brother with the sword and keeping his wrath for ever; and Ammon for ripping up the women with child of Gilead to enlarge their border. Fire is promised on the walls and palaces of every offender, and the people of Syria will go into captivity unto Kir.",
    context:"Amos introduces himself as a herdman of Tekoa whose words concerned Israel in the days of Uzziah king of Judah and Jeroboam the son of Joash.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"Amos 1:1", text:"The words of Amos, who was among the herdmen of Tekoa, which he saw concerning Israel in the days of Uzziah king of Judah, and in the days of Jeroboam the son of Joash king of Israel, two years before the earthquake." },
      { ref:"Amos 1:3", text:"Thus saith the LORD; For three transgressions of Damascus, and for four, I will not turn away the punishment thereof; because they have threshed Gilead with threshing instruments of iron:" },
      { ref:"Amos 1:11", text:"Thus saith the LORD; For three transgressions of Edom, and for four, I will not turn away the punishment thereof; because he did pursue his brother with the sword, and did cast off all pity, and his anger did tear perpetually, and he kept his wrath for ever:" }
    ],
    takeaway:"The Lord holds every nation accountable for its cruelty, and piled-up sins bring judgment that will not be turned away.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"Where does the book of Amos say its author came from?", choices:["Beth-el, where he served as a priest","Tekoa, where he was among the herdmen","Jerusalem, where he kept the temple gates","Gilead, where he raised cattle"], a:1, why:"Amos 1:1 identifies the speaker as one of the herdmen of Tekoa." },
      { q:"What does the repeating formula 'for three transgressions... and for four' convey about these nations?", choices:["The LORD kept an exact scorecard of seven crimes","Only the fourth offense was truly serious","Three nations were guilty and four were innocent","Their sins had piled up to fullness, so punishment would not be turned away"], a:3, why:"Each time the formula appears, the LORD immediately announces specific punishment — fire, captivity, or broken defenses — showing the sins had filled up." },
      { q:"What does Edom's sentence in Amos 1:11 teach about anger?", choices:["Anger that tears perpetually and keeps wrath for ever brings the LORD's judgment","Family loyalty cancels every wrong","Wrath is harmless if it stays private","Strong nations may settle old scores freely"], a:0, why:"Edom is condemned because he did pursue his brother with the sword, cast off all pity, and kept his wrath for ever." }
    ] },
  { n:2, summary:"The rota moves to Moab, condemned for burning the bones of the king of Edom into lime, then shocks its hearers by turning on Judah — despiser of the law of the LORD — and finally on Israel itself. Israel is charged with selling the righteous for silver and the poor for a pair of shoes, trampling the meek, profaning the holy name, and drinking the wine of the condemned in the house of their god. The LORD recalls His own acts: destroying the towering Amorite, bringing Israel up from Egypt, leading them forty years in the wilderness, and raising up prophets and Nazarites from their own sons. But they gave the Nazarites wine to drink and commanded the prophets, Prophesy not. So the LORD is pressed under them like a cart full of sheaves, and in that day neither the swift, the strong, nor the courageous shall deliver himself.",
    context:"The cycle of judgment turns from the surrounding nations to the covenant people as the LORD recalls the exodus and lists Israel's sins.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"Amos 2:4", text:"Thus saith the LORD; For three transgressions of Judah, and for four, I will not turn away the punishment thereof; because they have despised the law of the LORD, and have not kept his commandments, and their lies caused them to err, after the which their fathers have walked:" },
      { ref:"Amos 2:6", text:"Thus saith the LORD; For three transgressions of Israel, and for four, I will not turn away the punishment thereof; because they sold the righteous for silver, and the poor for a pair of shoes;" },
      { ref:"Amos 2:12", text:"But ye gave the Nazarites wine to drink; and commanded the prophets, saying, Prophesy not." }
    ],
    takeaway:"Covenant blessings raise responsibility: God's own people answer for despising His law and exploiting the poor.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"According to Amos 2:6, what had Israel sold the poor for?", choices:["A pair of shoes","A sack of barley","A plot of land","A jar of oil"], a:0, why:"Amos 2:6 says they sold the righteous for silver, and the poor for a pair of shoes." },
      { q:"Why was Israel's guilt worse than the other nations', even though the same formula introduces each?", choices:["Israel had a bigger army to defend itself","Israel had received the LORD's gifts — exodus deliverance, prophets, and Nazarites — and despised them","Israel had more cities to lose","The other nations had already paid for their crimes"], a:1, why:"Verses 9-12 recall destroyed Amorites, the exodus, forty years led through the wilderness, and raised-up prophets and Nazarites, whom Israel corrupted and silenced." },
      { q:"What does Amos 2 teach about the way God sees oppression of the poor?", choices:["It is a private economic matter","It offends only the victims themselves","It is a sin the LORD counts and judges, even among His covenant people","It is excused when laws allow it"], a:2, why:"The chapter opens Israel's indictment with selling the righteous for silver and the poor for a pair of shoes, and the LORD announces inescapable judgment." }
    ] },
  { n:3, summary:"The LORD speaks against the whole family He brought up from Egypt: you only have I known of all the families of the earth, therefore I will punish you for all your iniquities. Chosen status has sharpened accountability. A chain of plain questions shows that effects have causes — two cannot walk together except they be agreed, a lion roars only when it has prey, a trumpet stirs a city's fear, and no calamity comes to a city without the LORD. Above all, the Lord GOD will do nothing without revealing His secret to His servants the prophets, so Amos asks, who can but prophesy? Foreign powers are invited to witness Samaria's tumults and oppression, for the people know not to do right. An adversary will surround the land; Israel will be snatched out like scraps from a lion's mouth, Beth-el's altar horns will fall, and the great houses will have an end.",
    context:"The LORD explains why covenant Israel must be punished: unique nearness brings unique accountability, and prophets reveal His plans first.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"Amos 3:2", text:"You only have I known of all the families of the earth: therefore I will punish you for all your iniquities." },
      { ref:"Amos 3:3", text:"Can two walk together, except they be agreed?" },
      { ref:"Amos 3:7", text:"Surely the Lord GOD will do nothing, but he revealeth his secret unto his servants the prophets." }
    ],
    takeaway:"Because God reveals His will through His servants the prophets, He never brings judgment without first warning His people.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"Which two nations are invited to assemble on the mountains of Samaria and see its tumults?", choices:["Damascus and Gaza","Ashdod and Egypt","Tyrus and Edom","Assyria and Babylon"], a:1, why:"Amos 3:9 tells the palaces of Ashdod and the land of Egypt to assemble and behold the tumults and oppressed in Samaria." },
      { q:"According to Amos 3:2, why must Israel be punished for all its iniquities?", choices:["Because Israel had the most laws on its books","Because foreign nations had complained to the LORD","Because being the one family the LORD had known made its sins especially accountable","Because Israel had never offered sacrifices"], a:2, why:"The verse pairs election with accountability: you only have I known... therefore I will punish you for all your iniquities." },
      { q:"Amos 3:7 establishes what pattern about how God acts?", choices:["He acts without warning to keep people humble","He reveals His secret to His servants the prophets before He does anything","He changes course whenever nations protest","He works only through earthquakes and storms"], a:3, why:"Amos 3:7 says the Lord GOD will do nothing without revealing His secret to His servants the prophets." }
    ] },
  { n:4, summary:"Amos addresses the well-fed kine of Bashan on the mountain of Samaria — women who oppress the poor and crush the needy, telling their masters to bring them drink — and swears they will be led away with hooks. With sharp irony he invites them to keep doing religion their way: come to Beth-el and transgress, multiply transgression at Gilgal, offer the sacrifices that liketh you. Then comes the refrain that repeats six times: I gave you cleanness of teeth and want of bread — yet have ye not returned unto me. The LORD withheld rain city by city, sent blasting and mildew and the palmerworm, pestilence like Egypt's, war that slew the young men, and an overthrow like Sodom and Gomorrah — yet still they did not return. Because discipline failed, the next step is stark: prepare to meet thy God, O Israel — the former of mountains, the creator of wind, the LORD, the God of hosts.",
    context:"Repeated famine, drought, blight, pestilence, and war had failed to bring Israel back to the LORD, so a final confrontation is announced.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"Amos 4:6", text:"And I also have given you cleanness of teeth in all your cities, and want of bread in all your places: yet have ye not returned unto me, saith the LORD." },
      { ref:"Amos 4:12", text:"Therefore thus will I do unto thee, O Israel: and because I will do this unto thee, prepare to meet thy God, O Israel." },
      { ref:"Amos 4:13", text:"For, lo, he that formeth the mountains, and createth the wind, and declareth unto man what is his thought, that maketh the morning darkness, and treadeth upon the high places of the earth, The LORD, The God of hosts, is his name." }
    ],
    takeaway:"Hard experiences are God's invitations to return; whether we heed them or not, everyone must meet God.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"Amos compares some of Israel's overthrow to the destruction of which places?", choices:["Sodom and Gomorrah","Babylon and Nineveh","Jericho and Ai","Dan and Beer-sheba"], a:0, why:"Amos 4:11 says I have overthrown some of you, as God overthrew Sodom and Gomorrah." },
      { q:"In context, what does 'prepare to meet thy God, O Israel' mean?", choices:["Pack for a pilgrimage to Jerusalem","Get ready for a direct confrontation with the LORD after every lesser warning was ignored","Set a place at the table for an honored guest","Plan a festival to celebrate the harvest"], a:2, why:"After five refused warnings, Amos 4:12 announces that the LORD Himself will now act against Israel." },
      { q:"The repeated refrain 'yet have ye not returned unto me' teaches what about discipline and repentance?", choices:["Suffering automatically produces repentance","God stops calling after repeated failures","Prosperity is a surer sign of God's favor than hardship","God's repeated calls can be refused, but refusing them only hardens toward judgment"], a:3, why:"Six times the LORD names a chastening — hunger, drought, blight, pestilence, war, overthrow — and six times Israel did not return." }
    ] },
  { n:5, summary:"This lament for the fallen virgin of Israel sets two roads before the people. One is religion without righteousness: feasts, burnt offerings, and songs — worship the LORD says He hates and will not smell while judgment is turned to wormwood. The other road is life: the LORD commands, seek ye me, and ye shall live; seek Him, not the shrines of Beth-el and Gilgal that are headed for captivity. God, who made the seven stars and Orion, is the One offended by those who trample the poor, take bribes, and afflict the just in the gate. Amos pleads: hate the evil, love the good, establish judgment in the gate, and it may be the LORD will be gracious to the remnant of Joseph. Those who long for the day of the LORD are warned it will be darkness, not light; instead, let judgment run down as waters, and righteousness as a mighty stream.",
    context:"A funeral song for Israel exposes worship divorced from justice and pleads for a seeking of the LORD that could spare a remnant.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"Amos 5:4", text:"For thus saith the LORD unto the house of Israel, Seek ye me, and ye shall live:" },
      { ref:"Amos 5:14", text:"Seek good, and not evil, that ye may live: and so the LORD, the God of hosts, shall be with you, as ye have spoken." },
      { ref:"Amos 5:24", text:"But let judgment run down as waters, and righteousness as a mighty stream." }
    ],
    takeaway:"God wants justice and righteousness in daily life more than crowded rituals offered with unrepentant hearts.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"What two qualities does Amos 5:24 command to run down like waters?", choices:["Judgment and righteousness","Silver and gold","Corn and wine","Kindness and truth"], a:0, why:"Amos 5:24 reads, let judgment run down as waters, and righteousness as a mighty stream." },
      { q:"Why does the LORD say He hates and will not smell Israel's feast days and offerings?", choices:["The animals offered were blemished","The worshipers turned judgment to wormwood, afflicted the just, and trampled the poor","The festivals were held on the wrong dates","The people refused to sing loudly enough"], a:1, why:"Verses 7, 10-12 and 21-23 place the rejected feasts beside courts that hate upright rebuke and take bribes while treading on the poor." },
      { q:"What warning does Amos 5 give to those who desire the day of the LORD?", choices:["It will arrive later than expected","It is only a metaphor for good harvests","It belongs to foreign nations instead","For the unrepentant it is darkness, and not light"], a:3, why:"Amos 5:18-20 compares the day to fleeing a lion and meeting a bear — darkness with no brightness in it." }
    ] },
  { n:6, summary:"Woe is pronounced on the complacent elite of Zion and Samaria, the chieftains who feel secure at the head of the nations. They push the evil day far away while violence draws near, lounging on beds of ivory, stretching themselves on couches, eating lambs and stall-fed calves, inventing new instruments of music like David, drinking wine by the bowlful, and anointing themselves with the chief ointments — but they are not grieved for the affliction of Joseph. For that, they will go captive with the first of the captives, and their banquets will end. The LORD has sworn by Himself that He abhors the excellency of Jacob: ten men in one house will die, and the survivor will be hushed about naming the LORD. Their boast of winning horns by their own strength is hollow; God will raise up a nation to afflict them from Hemath to the river of the wilderness.",
    context:"The target is the wealthy, self-satisfied leadership of Israel and Judah whose luxury blinded them to the affliction of the nation.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"Amos 6:1", text:"Woe to them that are at ease in Zion, and trust in the mountain of Samaria, which are named chief of the nations, to whom the house of Israel came!" },
      { ref:"Amos 6:6", text:"That drink wine in bowls, and anoint themselves with the chief ointments: but they are not grieved for the affliction of Joseph." },
      { ref:"Amos 6:12", text:"Shall horses run upon the rock? will one plow there with oxen? for ye have turned judgment into gall, and the fruit of righteousness into hemlock:" }
    ],
    takeaway:"Comfort that numbs us to other people's affliction is not security; it is the first stage of collapse.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"On what furniture were the complacent leaders stretching themselves, according to Amos 6?", choices:["Beds of ivory","Chairs of gold","Couches of cedar","Cushions of wool"], a:0, why:"Amos 6:4 describes those who lie upon beds of ivory and stretch themselves upon their couches." },
      { q:"What was the elite's defining failure amid their luxury in Amos 6:6?", choices:["They forgot to hire skilled musicians","They were not grieved for the affliction of Joseph","They drank their wine undiluted","They stopped paying the tithe"], a:1, why:"The luxury list ends with the charge that they are not grieved for the affliction of Joseph." },
      { q:"What does Amos 6 teach about security built on wealth and ease?", choices:["Comfort proves the LORD's approval","Luxury protects a nation from judgment","Self-satisfied ease that ignores a nation's affliction invites the LORD's sworn opposition","Wealth is evil in every form"], a:2, why:"The woe opens on those at ease in Zion, and the LORD swears by Himself to abhor the excellency of Jacob and deliver up the city." }
    ] },
  { n:7, summary:"Amos sees three visions. First the LORD forms grasshoppers to eat the later grass, and Amos intercedes — forgive, I beseech thee: by whom shall Jacob arise? for he is small — and the LORD answers, It shall not be. Next fire devours the great deep; Amos pleads again, and the LORD relents a second time. But in the third vision the LORD stands on a wall made by a plumbline with a plumbline in His hand and announces that He is setting a plumbline in the midst of His people Israel; He will not again pass by them any more. Amaziah the priest of Beth-el reports Amos to King Jeroboam as a conspirator and orders the seer back to Judah, calling Beth-el the king's chapel. Amos answers that he was no prophet nor prophet's son but a herdman and gatherer of sycomore fruit whom the LORD took as he followed the flock and sent to prophesy to Israel — and judgment follows on Amaziah's house.",
    context:"Twice the prophet's intercession delays judgment, but the plumbline vision marks the point of no return, and the priest of Beth-el moves to silence Amos.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"Amos 7:8", text:"And the LORD said unto me, Amos, what seest thou? And I said, A plumbline. Then said the Lord, Behold, I will set a plumbline in the midst of my people Israel: I will not again pass by them any more:" },
      { ref:"Amos 7:14", text:"Then answered Amos, and said to Amaziah, I was no prophet, neither was I a prophet's son; but I was an herdman, and a gatherer of sycomore fruit:" },
      { ref:"Amos 7:15", text:"And the LORD took me as I followed the flock, and the LORD said unto me, Go, prophesy unto my people Israel." }
    ],
    takeaway:"Intercession can stay God's hand, but a people measured against God's plumbline must finally deal with what it reveals.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"How did Amos describe himself when he answered Amaziah?", choices:["A priest trained at Beth-el","A herdman and a gatherer of sycomore fruit","A palace scribe from Samaria","A prophet's son from Judah"], a:1, why:"Amos 7:14-15 says he was no prophet nor prophet's son but a herdman and gatherer of sycomore fruit whom the LORD took as he followed the flock." },
      { q:"Why does Amos pray, 'O Lord GOD, forgive... by whom shall Jacob arise? for he is small'?", choices:["He worries the locusts will spread to Judah","He fears the king will punish him for the vision","He knows Israel is too weak to survive the judgment he sees and pleads for mercy","He believes Jacob should first build larger cities"], a:2, why:"Facing the grasshoppers and the fire, Amos pleads that Jacob is small and cannot rise under the blow, and the LORD relents both times." },
      { q:"What does Amaziah's order to stop prophesying at Beth-el illustrate about speaking God's word?", choices:["Prophets should stay in their home districts","Royal sanctuaries outrank prophetic messages","True prophets soften their words under pressure","Faithfulness to God's word often provokes opposition from religious and political power"], a:3, why:"Amaziah calls Beth-el the king's chapel and court and orders Amos out, and the prophet answers with a harder word, not a softer one." }
    ] },
  { n:8, summary:"The fourth vision is a basket of summer fruit: just as ripe fruit is picked right before it spoils, the end is come upon Israel, and the LORD will not again pass by them. Behind the sentence stands a greed that treats holy days as obstacles to profit — when will the new moon be gone, that we may sell corn? — with small ephahs, great shekels, falsified balances, and the poor bought for silver and a pair of shoes. The LORD swears by the excellency of Jacob that He will never forget any of their works. The land will tremble, the sun will go down at noon, and feasts will turn to mourning like the grief for an only son. Worst of all comes a famine not of bread nor thirst for water, but of hearing the words of the LORD: people will run from sea to sea seeking it and not find it, and those who swear by Samaria's idols will fall never to rise.",
    context:"The summer-fruit vision fixes Israel's end and exposes dishonest trade while announcing a famine of hearing the words of the LORD.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"Amos 8:2", text:"And he said, Amos, what seest thou? And I said, A basket of summer fruit. Then said the LORD unto me, The end is come upon my people of Israel; I will not again pass by them any more." },
      { ref:"Amos 8:5", text:"Saying, When will the new moon be gone, that we may sell corn? and the sabbath, that we may set forth wheat, making the ephah small, and the shekel great, and falsifying the balances by deceit?" },
      { ref:"Amos 8:11", text:"Behold, the days come, saith the Lord GOD, that I will send a famine in the land, not a famine of bread, nor a thirst for water, but of hearing the words of the LORD:" }
    ],
    takeaway:"No loss is poorer than losing access to God's word; treasure every opportunity to hear it.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"What did Amos see in the vision that opens Amos 8?", choices:["A basket of summer fruit","A plumbline on a wall","Grasshoppers eating the grass","A fire devouring the great deep"], a:0, why:"Amos 8:1-2 records the vision of a basket of summer fruit and the word that the end is come upon Israel." },
      { q:"What famine does the LORD promise in Amos 8:11-12?", choices:["A failure of the wheat and barley harvest","A drought drying every well and cistern","A famine of hearing the words of the LORD","A shortage caused by merchants hoarding grain"], a:2, why:"The LORD sends a famine not of bread nor thirst for water, but of hearing the words of the LORD, sought from sea to sea and not found." },
      { q:"What does Amos 8 teach about dishonest business practices?", choices:["Clever dealing shows the LORD's blessing","Only physical violence counts as sin","Dishonest scales can be balanced by offerings","The LORD never forgets exploitation of the poor and brings it into judgment"], a:3, why:"Amos 8:7 says the LORD hath sworn by the excellency of Jacob, Surely I will never forget any of their works." }
    ] },
  { n:9, summary:"The book closes with the Lord standing at the altar and commanding a judgment no one escapes: though Israelites dig into hell, climb to heaven, hide on Carmel, sink in the bottom of the sea, or go into captivity, the hand of the LORD will find them. The God who calls the waters of the sea asks whether Israel is more to Him than the children of the Ethiopians — He moved Israel from Egypt, the Philistines from Caphtor, and the Syrians from Kir. The sinful kingdom will be destroyed, yet the house of Jacob will not be utterly destroyed: Israel will be sifted among all nations like corn in a sieve, yet the least grain shall not fall upon the earth. Then the turn: in that day the LORD will raise up the tabernacle of David that is fallen, build it as in the days of old, and plant His people in their land, never to be pulled up again.",
    context:"The final chapter joins inescapable judgment to restoration: a sifted Israel survives, and the fallen tabernacle of David will be raised again.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"Amos 9:9", text:"For, lo, I will command, and I will sift the house of Israel among all nations, like as corn is sifted in a sieve, yet shall not the least grain fall upon the earth." },
      { ref:"Amos 9:11", text:"In that day will I raise up the tabernacle of David that is fallen, and close up the breaches thereof; and I will raise up his ruins, and I will build it as in the days of old:" },
      { ref:"Amos 9:14", text:"And I will bring again the captivity of my people of Israel, and they shall build the waste cities, and inhabit them; and they shall plant vineyards, and drink the wine thereof; they shall also make gardens, and eat the fruit of them." }
    ],
    takeaway:"God's sifting preserves every true grain, and beyond judgment stands His promised restoration through the house of David.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"Which three movements does Amos 9:7 cite as the LORD's doing?", choices:["Israel from Egypt, Philistines from Caphtor, Syrians from Kir","Judah from Babylon, Edom from Bozrah, Moab from Kerioth","Israel from Egypt, Assyria from Elam, Judah from Jerusalem","Philistines from Egypt, Syrians from Damascus, Israel from Gilead"], a:0, why:"Amos 9:7 recalls Israel brought up out of Egypt, the Philistines from Caphtor, and the Syrians from Kir." },
      { q:"What does the promise to sift the house of Israel like corn in a sieve mean?", choices:["Israel would starve when the grain ran out","Israel would be scattered among the nations, yet the faithful would not be lost","A grain tax would fund the exile","The harvest would fail every year"], a:1, why:"Amos 9:8-9 pairs destruction of the sinful kingdom with a sifting among all nations in which not the least grain falls to the earth." },
      { q:"What does the promise to raise up the tabernacle of David that is fallen show about God's covenants?", choices:["Judgment cancels every earlier promise","God's restoration purposes survive the fall of a dynasty","Only military victories can rebuild what judgment tore down","The Davidic kingdom was never meant to endure"], a:3, why:"After announcing the sifting, Amos 9:11 turns to that day, when the LORD Himself will raise up and rebuild David's fallen tabernacle." }
    ] }
);
(_ob.chapters = _ob.chapters || []).push(
  { n:1, summary:"Obadiah's vision is aimed at Edom, the descendants of Esau who felt untouchable in the clefts of the rock. The pride of their heart has deceived them — who shall bring me down to the ground? — yet the LORD vows that though they set their nest as high as the stars, He will bring them down, and even Esau's hidden things will be searched out. Edom's worst crime was the day Jerusalem fell: they stood on the other side, rejoiced over the children of Judah in the day of their destruction, looted the city, and stood in the crossway to cut off the survivors. The rule of reaping follows: as thou hast done, it shall be done unto thee. But the vision ends in deliverance — mount Zion will be holy, the house of Jacob will be a fire and the house of Esau stubble, and saviours shall come up on mount Zion, for the kingdom shall be the LORD's.",
    context:"The book identifies itself only as the vision of Obadiah and delivers the word of the LORD against Edom, the nation tied throughout to Esau, Jacob's brother.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"Obadiah 1:3", text:"The pride of thine heart hath deceived thee, thou that dwellest in the clefts of the rock, whose habitation is high; that saith in his heart, Who shall bring me down to the ground?" },
      { ref:"Obadiah 1:15", text:"For the day of the LORD is near upon all the heathen: as thou hast done, it shall be done unto thee: thy reward shall return upon thine own head." },
      { ref:"Obadiah 1:21", text:"And saviours shall come up on mount Zion to judge the mount of Esau; and the kingdom shall be the LORD's." }
    ],
    takeaway:"Pride in unreachable strongholds cannot shield anyone from the Lord, who repays cruelty to His people in kind.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"Where did Edom's pride tell it no one could bring it down from?", choices:["In the clefts of the rock","Behind the river Euphrates","Inside the walls of Samaria","On the heights of Carmel"], a:0, why:"Obadiah 1:3 describes Edom dwelling in the clefts of the rock, whose habitation is high." },
      { q:"What made Edom's violence against 'thy brother Jacob' so grave?", choices:["Edom had besieged Jerusalem with its own army","When Jerusalem fell, Edom stood on the other side, rejoiced, looted, and cut off fugitives at the crossway","Edom had refused to trade grain with Judah","Edom had burned the temple records"], a:1, why:"Obadiah 1:11-14 lists standing on the other side, rejoicing in Judah's destruction, laying hands on their substance, and delivering up the escapees." },
      { q:"What does 'as thou hast done, it shall be done unto thee' teach about God's justice?", choices:["God punishes only open warfare","Justice is random across the nations","God repays people's treatment of others in kind, returning their reward on their own head","Time alone erases every wrong"], a:2, why:"Obadiah 1:15 states the reaping rule for the day of the LORD: the reward returns upon thine own head." }
    ] }
);
(_jn.chapters = _jn.chapters || []).push(
  { n:1, summary:"The word of the LORD comes to Jonah the son of Amittai: go to Nineveh, that great city, and cry against it, for its wickedness is come up before God. Instead Jonah flees toward Tarshish, pays the fare at Joppa, and boards a ship heading the other way. The LORD sends a great wind, and while the pagan mariners cry to their gods and hurl the cargo overboard, Jonah sleeps below. The lot falls on Jonah; he admits he is an Hebrew who fears the LORD, the God of heaven, which hath made the sea and the dry land, and that he is running from the LORD's presence. At his own request the sailors throw him overboard, the sea immediately calms, and the men offer sacrifice and vows to the LORD — but the LORD has prepared a great fish to swallow Jonah, and he is in its belly three days and three nights.",
    context:"The text names only Jonah the son of Amittai and sends him to Nineveh, called 'that great city,' whose wickedness had come up before God.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"Jonah 1:2", text:"Arise, go to Nineveh, that great city, and cry against it; for their wickedness is come up before me." },
      { ref:"Jonah 1:9", text:"And he said unto them, I am an Hebrew; and I fear the LORD, the God of heaven, which hath made the sea and the dry land." },
      { ref:"Jonah 1:17", text:"Now the LORD had prepared a great fish to swallow up Jonah. And Jonah was in the belly of the fish three days and three nights." }
    ],
    takeaway:"You can board a ship out of God's calling, but you cannot outrun His presence or His purposes.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"Where was the ship headed when Jonah boarded it?", choices:["Nineveh","Tarshish","Joppa","Caphtor"], a:1, why:"Jonah 1:3 says he found a ship going to Tarshish, paid the fare, and went down into it to flee from the presence of the LORD." },
      { q:"Why does Jonah tell the sailors to cast him into the sea?", choices:["He plans to swim to shore and continue his journey","The sailors demand a sacrifice to their gods","He knows the storm is for his sake and gives himself up so the crew may live","He wants to prove the LORD will send a fish"], a:2, why:"Jonah 1:12 says the sea will calm if they cast him forth, for he knows the great tempest is upon them for his sake." },
      { q:"What does the sailors' change — from crying each to his own god to fearing the LORD, sacrificing, and making vows — suggest?", choices:["Pagan sailors are beyond God's reach","Storms only deepen unbelief","Sacrifices were meaningless outside Israel","Encountering the LORD's power can awaken real reverence in unlikely people"], a:3, why:"When the sea ceased raging, the men feared the LORD exceedingly, offered a sacrifice, and made vows." }
    ] },
  { n:2, summary:"From inside the fish Jonah prays. His prayer looks back on the drowning he escaped: cast into the deep, surrounded by floods and waves, wrapped in seaweed, sinking to the bottoms of the mountains with the earth's bars around him forever. Even banished from God's sight, he resolves, 'Yet I will look again toward thy holy temple.' At the point where his soul fainted, he remembered the LORD, and his prayer reached God's holy temple. He draws the lesson that those who cling to lying vanities forsake their own mercy, and he vows a sacrifice of thanksgiving, declaring, 'Salvation is of the LORD.' Then the LORD speaks to the fish, and it vomits Jonah out onto dry land — a rescued man given a second chance.",
    context:"Jonah prays from inside the great fish, recounting his near drowning in the language of the deep and vowing thanksgiving to the LORD.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"Jonah 2:2", text:"And said, I cried by reason of mine affliction unto the LORD, and he heard me; out of the belly of hell cried I, and thou heardest my voice." },
      { ref:"Jonah 2:7", text:"When my soul fainted within me I remembered the LORD: and my prayer came in unto thee, into thine holy temple." },
      { ref:"Jonah 2:9", text:"But I will sacrifice unto thee with the voice of thanksgiving; I will pay that that I have vowed. Salvation is of the LORD." }
    ],
    takeaway:"Prayer from the lowest place still reaches God's holy temple; salvation belongs to the Lord.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"How long was Jonah in the belly of the great fish?", choices:["Three days and three nights","Seven days and seven nights","One night","Forty days"], a:0, why:"Jonah 1:17 records that Jonah was in the belly of the fish three days and three nights." },
      { q:"What does Jonah mean by 'They that observe lying vanities forsake their own mercy'?", choices:["Nature worship is the safest religion","Fishermen should return small catches to the sea","Clinging to false hopes and idols means abandoning the very mercy God gives","Mercy should be limited to one's own family"], a:2, why:"Jonah 2:8 contrasts trusting lying vanities with his own experience: crying to the LORD and being heard." },
      { q:"Jonah 2 models what truth about prayer from the lowest point of life?", choices:["God no longer hears those who ran from Him","Only offerings in the temple count as prayer","Complaints disqualify a prayer","Honest prayer from the depths is heard, and the LORD brings life up from corruption"], a:3, why:"Jonah cries from the deep, his prayer enters God's holy temple, and the LORD brings up his life from corruption." }
    ] },
  { n:3, summary:"When the word of the LORD comes to Jonah a second time with the same assignment, this time he obeys. Nineveh is an exceeding great city of three days' journey; after entering a day's journey, Jonah delivers one stark sermon: yet forty days, and Nineveh shall be overthrown. The response is total. The people believe God, proclaim a fast, and put on sackcloth from the greatest of them even to the least. When word reaches the king, he rises from his throne, lays aside his robe, covers himself with sackcloth, and sits in ashes; by decree of the king and his nobles, neither man nor beast tastes anything, and all must cry mightily unto God. The turning is real, for everyone must turn from his evil way and from the violence in their hands — and God sees their works, repents of the evil He threatened, and does it not.",
    context:"Nineveh repents wholesale at a one-line warning, from the king's throne and the nobles' decree down to the livestock covered in sackcloth.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"Jonah 3:4", text:"And Jonah began to enter into the city a day's journey, and he cried, and said, Yet forty days, and Nineveh shall be overthrown." },
      { ref:"Jonah 3:5", text:"So the people of Nineveh believed God, and proclaimed a fast, and put on sackcloth, from the greatest of them even to the least of them." },
      { ref:"Jonah 3:10", text:"And God saw their works, that they turned from their evil way; and God repented of the evil, that he had said that he would do unto them; and he did it not." }
    ],
    takeaway:"God's warnings are invitations: repentance that turns from evil can stay even an announced judgment.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"How many days' warning did Jonah's message give Nineveh before its overthrow?", choices:["Forty days","Seven days","Three days","A full year"], a:0, why:"Jonah 3:4 cries, Yet forty days, and Nineveh shall be overthrown." },
      { q:"What shows Nineveh's repentance went deeper than outward signs?", choices:["The city commissioned new monuments to its gods","The king and people turned every one from his evil way and from the violence in their hands","They taxed themselves to pay for the fast","They exiled Jonah from the city"], a:1, why:"The royal decree requires crying mightily to God and turning from evil ways and violence, and God saw their works that they turned from their evil way." },
      { q:"What does God's response in Jonah 3:10 reveal about the purpose of warnings of judgment?", choices:["They are fixed verdicts that must fall regardless","They exist mainly to display God's anger","They apply only to Israelite audiences","They are calls to repent that, when heeded, move God to turn from the threatened evil"], a:3, why:"When God saw that Nineveh turned from its evil way, He repented of the evil He had said He would do, and did it not." }
    ] },
  { n:4, summary:"Nineveh's repentance makes Jonah furious, and he prays that this is exactly why he fled to Tarshish: he knew the LORD is gracious, merciful, slow to anger, of great kindness, and one who repents of the evil — and he asks God to take his life. The LORD answers with a question: doest thou well to be angry? Jonah camps east of the city to see what will become of it. God prepares a gourd to shade him, and Jonah is exceeding glad; then God sends a worm to smite the gourd, a vehement east wind, and a beating sun until Jonah again wishes to die. The LORD draws the lesson: Jonah pitied a plant he never labored over, one that came up in a night and perished in a night — should not God spare Nineveh, that great city, with more than sixscore thousand souls and much cattle besides?",
    context:"Jonah camps outside Nineveh hoping for its destruction, and the LORD teaches him a lesson about mercy with a gourd, a worm, and an east wind.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"Jonah 4:2", text:"And he prayed unto the LORD, and said, I pray thee, O LORD, was not this my saying, when I was yet in my country? Therefore I fled before unto Tarshish: for I knew that thou art a gracious God, and merciful, slow to anger, and of great kindness, and repentest thee of the evil." },
      { ref:"Jonah 4:4", text:"Then said the LORD, Doest thou well to be angry?" },
      { ref:"Jonah 4:11", text:"And should not I spare Nineveh, that great city, wherein are more than sixscore thousand persons that cannot discern between their right hand and their left hand; and also much cattle?" }
    ],
    takeaway:"God's mercy is wider than our grudges — He pities even the people and cities we would rather see condemned.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"How many persons did the LORD say were in Nineveh who could not discern between their right hand and their left hand?", choices:["More than sixscore thousand","About ten thousand","Nearly two hundred","Exactly nine thousand"], a:0, why:"Jonah 4:11 says more than sixscore thousand persons, plus much cattle." },
      { q:"What is the point of the gourd lesson the LORD gives Jonah?", choices:["Booths are better shelters than gourds","Jonah pitied a plant he never made while begrudging mercy to a great city full of people and cattle God made","Jonah should have asked the city to plant shade trees","The east wind proves Jonah's anger was justified"], a:1, why:"Jonah 4:10-11 contrasts Jonah's pity for a gourd he did not labor over with the LORD's right to spare Nineveh." },
      { q:"What does Jonah 4 teach about God's compassion toward people we may resent?", choices:["Compassion is reserved for the obedient","God rescinds mercy when prophets object","God's pity extends even to a wicked great city and its cattle, beyond the prophet's grudges","Mercy must be earned before it is given"], a:2, why:"The closing question — should not I spare Nineveh? — shows the LORD's sparing kindness reaching even the city Jonah wanted destroyed." }
    ] }
);
